# OpenSTA uses C++20 three-way comparison (operator<=>) on std::string in
# network/Network.cc. The libc++ in older macOS SDKs (e.g. the Ventura
# Command Line Tools, MacOSX13.sdk) lacks operator<=> for std::basic_string,
# so the build fails. Replace those three sites with std::string::compare(),
# which has identical sign semantics and needs no newer standard library.
patchfiles-append   patch-Network-string-spaceship.diff

# write_path_spice gave a sequential cell's asynchronous set/reset pins no
# value, so they fell through to the tie low default in
# writeSubcktInstVoltSrcs() -- which for an active low set/reset is the
# ASSERTED level. The flop output is then held at a constant, never makes the
# transition the reported path took, and the deck's arrival measurements come
# up empty: every path launching from such a flop yields an unusable deck.
# Deassert those pins from the complement of the liberty clear/preset
# expressions instead. Submitted upstream as parallaxsw/OpenSTA issue #480;
# DROP this patch once the pinned commit above includes the fix (it will then
# fail to apply). Sibling of the #474/#475 non-unate side input fix, which is
# already in the pinned commit.
#
# Source only: the upstream change also adds test/write_path_spice_async_set_reset
# (a gzipped liberty among other files), which a unified diff cannot carry and
# this port does not run anyway.
patchfiles-append   patch-WriteSpice-async-set-reset.diff

# Sibling defect to the one above, on the DATA side rather than the async
# set/reset side. write_path_spice chose a value for only ONE port of a
# sequential cell's next state expression: seqPortValues() called onePort(),
# which returns the first port it finds. For an enabled flop, next state is
# "(D & DE) | (IQ & !DE)" -- onePort() picks D, DE is never set, and DE falls
# through to the tie low default, so the enable is INACTIVE. The flop holds,
# the output never makes the transition the reported path took, and the deck's
# arrival measurements come up empty (7 of 15 paths on a sky130 picorv32,
# every one of them launching from an edfxtp).
#
# Now every settable input port of the next state gets the value that drives
# the output to the level the path needs. Cubes are taken under UNIVERSAL
# abstraction of the cell's internal state nodes (IQ/IQN): those cannot be
# driven in a deck, so a usable assignment has to hold for either value of
# them. That is what rejects the {DE=0} cubes -- they only reach the wanted
# level when the flop already happens to hold it -- and selects {D=1, DE=1}
# for a rise and {D=0, DE=1} for a fall.
#
# Not upstream: submitted, no response on the previous write_path_spice PR.
# Regression check: run write_path_spice over a design that uses enabled flops
# (e.g. sky130 picorv32, whose edfxtp cells hit this); every deck must produce
# its arrival measurements.
patchfiles-append   patch-WriteSpice-seq-enable.diff

# report_power aborted on any library whose power_lut_template names its slew
# axis input_net_transition rather than input_transition_time -- both are used
# in the wild, and some characterization tools emit the former:
#
#   Critical 226: liberty/InternalPower.cc line 171, unsupported table axes
#
# InternalPowerModel::axisValue() accepted only input_transition_time, while
# the sibling GateTableModel::axisValue() has always accepted the pair, which
# is why timing reads the same library without complaint and only power dies.
# Nothing rejects the table at read time either: InternalPowerModel::checkAxes()
# is never called from LibertyReader (unlike the gate/check/receiver models),
# and its axis list was the timing-check set, disjoint from what axisValue()
# evaluates -- so the two could never agree. This accepts the alias and
# realigns checkAxis() with what axisValue() actually supports.
#
# NOT submitted upstream. Drop this patch if a future pinned commit fixes it
# (it will then fail to apply).
patchfiles-append   patch-InternalPower-power-lut-axes.diff

# report_checks SIGSEGVs in the arnoldi delay calculator on a large design --
# ~976k cells with a 505 MB detailed (un-reduced) SPEF. read_liberty,
# read_verilog, link_design, read_sdc and read_spef all succeed; the crash is
# in the reducer, and the SAME design with the DEFAULT delay calculator reports
# fine, so it is arnoldi-specific rather than a size or memory limit (peak RSS
# 2.8 GB, deterministic across runs):
#
#   sta::ArnoldiReduce::setTerms(sta::ts_point *)
#   sta::ArnoldiReduce::makeRcmodelDrv()
#   sta::ArnoldiDelayCalc::findParasitic(...)
#   ... sta::Sta::findPathEnds(...)
#
# Three things in dcalc/ArnoldiReduce.cc combine. (1) The reset loop in
# loadWork() clears node_/eN/is_term but NOT tindex, and ts_pointV is a reused
# malloc'd pool, so internal nodes carry stale indices rather than zero.
# (2) findPt() cannot report failure: pt_map_ is read with operator[], which
# default-inserts 0 for an absent key, so a missing node silently yields
# &ts_pointV[0] -- normally an internal node, i.e. exactly the uninitialised
# case. (3) makeRcmodelDrv() checks neither the node nor the point before
# setTerms() uses tindex raw as an offset: `termV[0] = pdrv->tindex` then
# `p = pterm0 + termV[k]; outV[k] = p->ts` -- a wild read. It fires when a net's
# driver pin has no terminal point in that net's parasitic network, which is
# rare enough to need a very large design to hit with certainty.
#
# The patch initialises tindex to -1 so the condition is deterministic instead
# of depending on pool contents, and declines to reduce (returns nullptr, the
# function's existing "no reduced model" path, used three lines below for a
# degenerate ctot_) when the driver node is absent or is not a terminal.
#
# NOT submitted upstream, and NOT fixed there: ArnoldiReduce.cc is untouched
# since 2026-04-22, which predates the pinned commit, and no upstream issue or
# PR mentions arnoldi. Upstream did take the same shape of fix for the sibling
# calculator in ef0b6909 ("PrimaDelayCalc: handle degenerate parasitic
# networks", #476). Drop this patch if a future pinned commit fixes it.
#
# UNPROVEN: applies cleanly and the guard is inert wherever tindex was valid,
# but the regression that matters -- that designs which already reduce produce
# byte-identical report_checks output -- has not been measured. If the guard
# starts declining reduction on nets that previously reduced, delays shift
# SILENTLY, which is worse than the crash.
patchfiles-append   patch-ArnoldiReduce-null-driver-term.diff

# LVF sigma on CHECK (setup/hold) arcs is silently dropped. When reading a
# constraint arc's LVF tables, LibertyReader asks for
#     ocv_sigma_{rise,fall}_constraiint      (and _std_dev_, _mean_shift_,
#                                             _skewness_ -- four names)
# -- "constraiint", double i. Liberty files spell it _constraint, so the lookup
# never matches, no warning is issued, and with sta_pocv_mode normal or
# skew_normal setup/hold slack comes out as if the check arcs carried no
# statistical data at all. The delay-arc call just above (ocv_sigma_*_transition
# etc.) is spelled correctly and is not touched.
#
# POCV here is the OpenSTA 3.x RUNTIME mode, not the 2.x compile-time SSTA build:
# DelayNormal / DelaySkewNormal / PocvMode are always compiled in, and it is
# switched on with `set sta_pocv_mode normal` (or skew_normal) plus
# sta_pocv_quantile. The old set_pocv_sigma_factor command no longer exists, and
# the commands live in the sta:: namespace, so `info commands *pocv*` at global
# scope finds nothing -- that is not evidence of a non-POCV build.
#
# NOT fixed upstream: the misspelling is still present at parallaxsw master.
# NOT submitted upstream. Drop this patch if a future pinned commit fixes it.
patchfiles-append   patch-LibertyReader-lvf-constraint-spelling.diff

# Under POCV every slack reporter except report_checks returns the wrong value,
# so a violating design reports clean. Statistical slack is (mean, sigma) and
# its pessimistic single value is ALWAYS mean - q*sigma -- low slack is the bad
# direction for setup and hold alike. ReportPath::reportSlack does that
# (EarlyLate::early()); two other paths do not:
#
#   WRONG SIGN. search/Search.i converts slack with delayAsFloat(slack,
#   min_max, sta) at seven sites (worst_slack_cmd/_scene,
#   total_negative_slack_cmd/_scene, endpoint_slack, PathEnd::slack,
#   Path::slack). For -max, MinMax::max() read as an EarlyLate is LATE, so
#   DelayOpsNormal::asFloat returns mean + q*sigma, the optimistic tail. That
#   feeds report_worst_slack, report_wns, report_tns, worst_slack and
#   total_negative_slack. The comparisons that pick the worst endpoint already
#   use early, so the right endpoints were chosen and then reported wrongly.
#
#   SIGMA IGNORED. get_property ... slack (pin slack_max/_min/..., path_end and
#   path slack) went through Properties::delayPropertyValue, which uses the
#   one-argument delayAsFloat() = mean. A new slackPropertyValue() converts with
#   early; delayPropertyValue itself is left alone because arrival/required
#   properties need different tails.
#
# Measured on a one-violator fixture (one setup path), POCV normal, q=3:
#                         unpatched      patched
#   report_checks         -0.436396     -0.436396   (unchanged, was right)
#   report_worst_slack    +0.836396     -0.436396
#   report_wns             0.000000     -0.436396
#   report_tns            +0.836396     -0.436396
#   get_property slack    +0.200000     -0.436396
# Scalar mode (no sigma) is unaffected: early == late == mean there.
#
# NOT fixed upstream (present at parallaxsw master 7a034b56); not submitted.
# Same Search.i also converts PathEnd/Path `required` with minMax (lines
# ~1174/1224) where ReportPath uses the opposite tail -- probably the same
# class of bug, NOT addressed or tested here.
patchfiles-append   patch-POCV-slack-pessimistic-tail.diff

# CCS output-current waveforms are silently misread when the library's
# output_current_template spells its slew axis input_transition_time rather
# than input_net_transition. LibertyReader::readOutputWaveforms decides which
# table axis is slew by testing axis1 == input_net_transition ONLY; anything
# else falls into the else branch, which takes axis2 as slew and axis1 as load
# cap -- a silent transpose, no warning. GateTableModel::axisValue has always
# accepted both spellings, so delay tables read fine and only the waveform
# path is affected, i.e. the ccs_ceff and prima delay calculators.
#
# Measured, one INV with an RC net from SPEF, ccs_ceff, rise delay at u1/Z:
#                      input_net_transition   input_transition_time
#   unpatched                0.039784               0.046464
#   patched                  0.039784               0.039784
# Unpatched, the misspelled-axis library quietly lands on (near) the NLDM
# answer instead of using its CCS data -- a 17% difference on this fixture.
# dmp_ceff_elmore (NLDM) is identical across all four, as it should be.
#
# The library-side spelling is the Liberty-correct one for CCS templates, so
# this is tool robustness, consistent with GateTableModel. The related
# OutputWaveforms::checkAxes is never called; NOTE if anyone wires it up that it
# dereferences axis2/axis3 without null checks and would crash on a 2-D
# template. Not fixed upstream (present at parallaxsw master); not submitted.
patchfiles-append   patch-LibertyReader-ccs-slew-axis-spelling.diff

# The prima delay calculator SIGSEGVs when a driver pin is absent from its net's
# parasitic network -- the same failure class as ArnoldiReduce above.
# PrimaDelayCalc::findNodeCount() places a pin in pin_node_map_ only if its node
# exists, is not external, and (since upstream ef0b6909) has a resistive path to
# a driver. stampEqns(), updateCeffIdrvr() and dcalcResults() then read that map
# with operator[], which default-inserts index 0 for a missing pin. With no node
# placed at all, node_count_ is 0, so the G/B stamps and threshold_times_[0] go
# out of bounds; with other nodes placed the pin is silently aliased onto node 0
# (for an unreachable load: zero wire delay, driver slew).
#
# The patch checks every driver and load pin is placed before stamping, and
# otherwise declines exactly as a checkArgs() failure does -- tableDcalcResults()
# hands the net to the table delay calculator. The four indexing reads become
# .at() (a future violation throws rather than default-inserting), and
# voltage(const Pin*) -- debug and watch-pin only -- uses find().
#
# Measured, one INV, SPEF variants, set_delay_calculator prima:
#   driver absent from net z     unpatched SIGSEGV (rc 139)   patched 0.046677
#   load with no resistive path  unpatched 0.086665           patched 0.039994
#   well-formed net              byte-identical output before/after
# All other calculators byte-identical before/after on all three. A crash was
# produced, so this is a fixed bug, not just a hardening.
#
# NOT fixed upstream; not submitted.
patchfiles-append   patch-PrimaDelayCalc-pin-not-in-network.diff

# The four patches below fix write_path_spice decks so the path in the deck
# switches as it was timed. Found and checked against icasta's own
# write_path_spice (icasta BUGS-FIXED.md 32-35), on the routed sky130 6502
# (riscvpr work_sky130_mos6502) and the s7 6502 (work_mos6502_ccs), and with
# riscvpr's scripts/polarity_audit.py. They change the decks, so SPICE numbers
# from decks written after this revision are not comparable with earlier ones
# where the tied pins or the first stage's net differ.

# write_path_spice SIGSEGVs on a path with no gate on it: an input port wired
# straight to a register pin (routed sky130 6502, -path_delay min: NMI ->
# _2023_/D), after writing the decks before it (rc 139).
# writeInputWaveform()/writeClkWaveform() ask stageGateArc(input_stage + 1)
# for the next arc's slew axis; on a two-pin path PathExpanded::path() is null
# past the end and stageGateArc() dereferences it. The guard returns no arc,
# so findSlew() falls back as it already does (one time unit when the port has
# no slew). Decks of other paths are byte-identical before/after.
# NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-WritePathSpice-no-gate-path.diff

# write_path_spice fails with error 1605 on a cell subckt file whose cells
# instantiate their transistors as subckts, as every sky130 cell does
# ("X0 ... sky130_fd_pr__nfet_01v8 w=... l=..."):
#
#   Error: 1605 The subkct file ... is missing definitions for
#   .ends
#   l=150000u
#
# findCellSubckts() took the last word of the line AFTER each X statement (and
# of continuation lines) as the called subckt, so parameters and ".ends"
# became cell names; it also scanned the file once in order, missing a subckt
# defined before the cell that calls it. riscvpr's flow works around it by
# appending dummy ".subckt l=..." definitions and stripping them from the
# decks afterwards. Now the called subckt is the X statement's own last word
# that is not a name=value parameter (continuation lines joined), found to a
# fixed point, and only the path's own cells must be in the file: what they
# instantiate and the file does not define (the PDK's transistors) is the
# model file's. With the dummies removed, the 6502 decks are byte-identical to
# the ones written with them; the subckt sidecar lacks only the dummies.
# NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-WriteSpice-nested-subckts.diff

# Three more tie shapes that freeze or misroute the path in the deck
# (riscvpr polarity_findings shapes 4-5 and the load ties its audit flags):
#
#  - An integrated clock gate on the path has its enable tied OFF, so the
#    gated clock never pulses (riscvpr cv32e40p, 0 of 15 paths measured).
#    gatePortValues() sensitized the gated clock's function, whose enable
#    term is the gate's latched state -- IQ, or for sky130's dlclkp/sdlclkp a
#    statetable node M0 with no GCLK function at all -- which no deck can
#    drive, so the enable fell to the tie low. Now a clock gate's
#    clock_gate_enable_pin is tied on (a test enable keeps the tie low).
#  - A scan flop launches through its scan path: CUDD's first cube of
#    "(D&!SCE) | (SCD&SCE)" is SCE=1, SCD carrying the edge. Next-state
#    cubes are now chosen with the fewest inputs high (functional mode: an
#    enable on, a scan enable off), first in CUDD's order on ties.
#  - Every input of a path net's other loads was tied low, holding a load
#    register in reset (sky130 dfrtp RESET_B) and an enable flop's enable off
#    (which also changes the load its D pin puts on the net). Loads are now in
#    functional mode: a clock gate's enable on, a sequential's next state
#    following its data input (the load pin if it is one), set and reset
#    deasserted. Loads are still not sensitized.
#
# polarity_audit.py on the routed sky130 CTS 6502 (21 decks): unpatched 15
# flipped, 87 seqbad; patched 0, 0. On the s7 6502 (30 decks, 22 non-unate
# gates): 0, 0 both. NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-WriteSpice-functional-ties.diff

# The first stage's net (from an input port) had none of its other loads:
# writeInputStage() wrote the net's parasitics only, though the timing loads
# the port with every pin on it. On the routed sky130 6502 the wire from RDY
# (112 other loads) to _0932_/S simulated at 7.2 ps in ngspice against
# 13.7 ps timed; with its loads, 13.2 ps. The input stage now writes its loads
# as a gate stage does, unless the input is an IDEAL clock: the timing takes
# its wires as ideal, and its net can reach every register of the design
# (riscvpr found thousands of load flops make a deck take hours). A propagated
# clock's first net (clock port -> root buffer) does get its loads.
# NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-WritePathSpice-input-stage-loads.diff

# get_property <pin> is_clock segfaulted on a pin with no liberty port -- a
# top-level port's pin, as `get_property [get_clocks clk] sources` returns.
# Properties::getProperty(const Pin*) called isClock() on libertyPort(pin)
# without the null check its is_register_clock branch has. Now false, as
# is_register_clock. NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-Property-pin-is-clock-no-liberty-port.diff

# A port's slew_max and slew_min properties were its FALL slew only:
# Properties::getProperty(const Port*) passed RiseFallBoth::fall() where the
# pin's properties pass riseFall(). On the sky130 6502 with flow.sdc,
# AB[0] slew_max read 0.030507 though its rise slew is 0.075154. Now the
# worst of both edges, as a pin's. NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-Property-port-slew-both-edges.diff

# An arrival_* property read before anything had built the timing graph
# (get_property or -filter straight after read_sdc) segfaulted:
# Sta::arrival(const Pin*) called graph_->pinVertices() on a null graph; the
# Vertex overload it calls next runs searchPreamble(), too late. Now
# ensureGraph() first, as Sta::slack(const Pin*) already does.
# NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-Sta-arrival-ensure-graph.diff

# Slack at a pin (get_property slack_max/_min/..., Sta::slack) showed
# violations where no constrained path goes through it. Every Path starts
# with required_ 0, and the required search only visits vertices whose
# fanout required times changed: a path it never reaches keeps 0, so its
# slack is 0 - arrival, and a fanin taking the tightest of its fanouts'
# required times takes that 0, stops changing and is never visited
# itself. On the sky130 6502 with a false path -through _0958_/Y,
# _0958_/A read slack_max -0.514989 though report_checks -through it
# finds no paths, and _2017_/Q read -0.344927 though report_checks -from
# _2017_/CLK gives its paths 3.85. A vertex's paths now start with no
# required time (INF for max, -INF for min, RequiredCmp's init value).
# NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-TagGroup-required-init-value.diff

# On an ideal clock, slack on the clock network was less than that of
# anything it launches, by the network's delay to the pin: a register
# launches from the clock edge (clkPathArrival), but Path::slack takes the
# clock path's arrival, which includes the clock buffers' delays. On the
# sky130 6502, _1951_/CLK read slack_max 6.560971 though the worst path it
# launches has 6.999876 (the clock tree's 0.438904 less), so a
# slack-driven filter flags clock pins with no violation behind them. The
# required time carried back through a register clk->Q edge is now moved
# by the difference between the two arrivals. No change with propagated
# clocks. NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-Search-required-ideal-clk-launch.diff

# get_property $path_end endpoint_clock_pin segfaulted on an end with no
# capturing clock path (an output delay end): Properties::getProperty
# (PathEnd*) called pin() on a null targetClkPath(). Now NULL.
# NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-Property-path-end-no-target-clk-path.diff

# get_property $point required / slack on a path end's points read a
# required time nothing had set: find_timing_paths never runs the required
# search, so every point showed -INF or INF (0 before revision 15) and slack
# INF. The required search now runs first, on the search state the path
# belongs to (Search::findRequireds, not Sta::findRequired, whose
# searchPreamble would delete the path ends the point came from).
# NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-Property-path-required-search.diff

# The path ends find_timing_paths returns were freed by the next command
# that runs Sta::searchPreamble -- a pin's slack, report_tns, any arrival
# query -- although Search::findPathEnds documents them as kept until the
# next call; using the handles afterwards read freed memory (on the sky130
# 6502, get_property [get_pins _1889_/A] slack_max between two
# get_property $path_end startpoint segfaults). searchPreamble now deletes
# them only when they point to the filtered arrivals of a -from/-through
# search, which it deletes too, and makePathGroups deletes the previous set.
# NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-Search-keep-unfiltered-path-ends.diff

# report_check_types laid its reports out unevenly: a blank line after every
# capacitance check, inside the table too, while verbose slew, fanout and
# max skew blocks ran together with none between them. Each verbose block
# and each table now ends with one blank line. Report text only.
# NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-Sta-check-report-blank-lines.diff

# On an ideal clock, minimum pulse widths and maximum skews took the clock
# tree's buffer delays, which an ideal clock is defined not to have
# (clkPathArrival applies the ideal edge only at register clock pins): a
# pulse through two buffers of different rise and fall delays came out
# narrower than the waveform, and the skew report showed no network delay
# at one pin and the tree's at the other. New Search::clkCheckArrival gives
# an ideal clock's edge, insertion and latency at any pin; the checks and
# the skew report use it. No change with propagated clocks.
# NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-Search-ideal-clk-check-arrival.diff

# set_max_transition -clock_path never applied, and on a propagated clock
# network the clock's -data_path limit did instead: CheckSlews::findLimit
# chose by isIdealClock(pin), false for every pin it checks (ideal clock
# pins are skipped). Now by isClock(pin). NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-CheckSlews-clock-path-limit.diff

# set_max_capacitance on a pin was accepted and written by write_sdc but
# never checked: CheckCapacitances::findLimit asked the design, port and
# cell only. The pin's limit now counts (Sdc::capacitanceLimit takes a
# const Pin*). NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-CheckCapacitances-pin-limit.diff

# A library's default_max_capacitance was never read (only LibertyWriter
# wrote it), so output pins without max_capacitance had no limit. Read like
# default_max_transition; applied to max checks only (the internal pin
# branch of CheckCapacitances::findLimit lacked that test, harmless while
# the value was never set). NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-LibertyReader-default-max-capacitance.diff

# set_min_pulse_width -high (or -low) on a pin, instance or clock hid the
# other pulse's broader values: Sdc::minPulseWidth stopped at the first
# object with a value for either pulse, so after set_min_pulse_width -low
# 1.6 and set_min_pulse_width -high 1.0 [get_cells r1], r1's low pulse fell
# to the library's width. Each pulse now falls through pin, instance,
# clock and design on its own. NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-Sdc-min-pulse-width-per-edge.diff

# write_sdc dropped set_max_fanout (and set_min_fanout) on ports when the
# design had a limit of its own, so a written SDC read back checked those
# ports against the design's. Both are written. NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-WriteSdc-port-fanout-limits.diff

# write_sdc dropped set_min_pulse_width with no objects (the design-wide
# widths). Written after the pin, instance and clock widths.
# NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-WriteSdc-global-min-pulse-width.diff

# report_clock_latency counted a propagated clock's source latency twice:
# ClkDelays::delay took the arrival less the edge time as the network
# latency, though the arrival holds the source latency already, and
# setLatency added the insertion again (on a test design with
# set_clock_latency -source 0.5, a register at 0.80 ns showed 1.30 ns).
# NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-ClkLatency-source-latency-once.diff

# On an ideal clock, report_clock_latency and report_clock_skew took the
# clock tree's buffer delays and left out set_clock_latency: the network
# latency and the skew now use Search::clkCheckArrival (revision 17), the
# ideal edge, insertion and latency at any pin. No change with propagated
# clocks. NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-Search-ideal-clk-latency-skew.diff

# report_clock_skew kept the skews of its first report while the timing
# changed (ClkSkews::findClkSkew returned the stored skews when the clocks
# and scenes were the same), so later reports showed stale values on
# paths the timing update had freed. The skews are found for each report.
# NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-ClkSkew-find-skews-each-report.diff

# report_clock_skew -clocks always failed: the proc printed a debug line
# and then read $clks unset. It now takes the clocks given.
# NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-Search-report-clock-skew-clocks.diff

# A min_clock_tree_path/max_clock_tree_path table with no timing_sense was
# ignored by -include_internal_latency: an unknown sense makes two arcs to
# each edge, LibertyPort::clkTreeDelay took arcTo(rf), the opposite edge's,
# and rejected it. The arc from and to the same edge is used.
# NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-Liberty-clk-tree-delay-any-sense.diff

# report_lib_cell segfaulted on a cell with a clock tree path table:
# TimingArcSet::to_string named the from port, which those sets do not
# have. Listed as "-> CK". NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-TimingArc-clock-tree-path-to-string.diff

# get_timing_edges -of_objects returned a list of lists, one per instance
# or Liberty cell (get_timing_arcs_objects lappended each object's list), so
# foreach handed get_property a list ("... is not an object"). One list.
# NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-Sta-tcl-timing-edges-of-objects-flat.diff

# write_sdc wrote a disabled Liberty timing arc set as set_disable_timing
# [get_timing_edges -of_objects [get_lib_cells ...]], which read back
# disables every arc set of the cell. Each arc set is written by its from
# and to ports (-from/-to on the Liberty cell). NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-WriteSdc-disabled-arc-sets-by-ports.diff

# get_full_name / get_property full_name of a clock tree path arc set
# (min/max_clock_tree_path, which has no from port) segfaulted:
# TimingArcSet::libertyCell() went through the from port and returned
# null. It falls back to the to port, and the name has an empty from
# port. NOT submitted upstream.
patchfiles-append   patch-TimingArcSet-clock-tree-path-cell.diff

# OpenSTA is C++20 and its headers rely on P0634 ("Down with typename!"), which
# lets `typename` be omitted in dependent contexts -- e.g. include/sta/Iterator.hh
# declaring VECTOR_TYPE::const_iterator without it. clang only implemented P0634
# in clang 16, which Apple ships as AppleClang 15. On a machine whose compiler is
# older the build dies with dozens of:
#   include/sta/Iterator.hh:64:3: error: missing 'typename' prior to dependent
#   type name 'VECTOR_TYPE::const_iterator'
#
# This is easy to misdiagnose because it does NOT track the OS version: two
# macOS 13.7 boxes differed only in that one had Xcode.app (AppleClang 15) while
# the other had just the Command Line Tools (AppleClang 14.0.3) -- same OS, same
# SDK, same pinned commit, and only the CLT-only machine failed. Note also that
# `/usr/bin/clang++ --version` in a login shell can report the CLT compiler while
# MacPorts' build environment resolves to Xcode's, so trust the
# "CXX compiler identification" line in the build log, not the shell.
#
# Blacklisting instead of hardcoding a compiler: MacPorts then keeps using the
# system compiler wherever it is new enough, and only falls back to a MacPorts
# clang (adding the matching build dependency itself) where it is not.
compiler.blacklist-append {clang < 1500}

# bison (>= 3.2), flex and swig (>= 3.0, with the Tcl backend) generate the
# parsers and Tcl bindings at build time. The macOS system bison/flex are too
# old, so the MacPorts copies are used explicitly below.
depends_build-append \
                    port:bison \
                    port:flex \
                    port:swig \
                    port:swig-tcl

# tcl       command interpreter (required)
# eigen3    linear algebra (required, header-only)
# libfmt10  provides the fmt library OpenSTA uses when the toolchain lacks
#           std::format. Without it OpenSTA's CMake clones fmt via FetchContent
#           (network mid-build) and then installs that fmt into the prefix,
#           squatting on the canonical fmt paths. See fmt_DIR below.
depends_lib-append  port:tcl \
                    port:eigen3 \
                    port:libfmt10

# OpenSTA's bundled cmake/FindTCL.cmake hard-codes Homebrew search paths on
# macOS and sets NO_DEFAULT_PATH, so it cannot locate the MacPorts Tcl (or
# tclreadline) unless pointed at ${prefix}/lib explicitly.
#
# Tcl readline support is forced OFF here so the build does not silently link a
# tclreadline that happens to be installed without declaring it; the +readline
# variant turns it on with the dependency declared.
# The MacPorts libfmt10 port installs its CMake package config into a versioned
# subdir (${prefix}/lib/libfmt10/cmake/fmt) that is off CMake's default search
# path, so find_package(fmt) misses it and OpenSTA falls back to FetchContent.
# Point fmt_DIR at it directly so the installed fmt is used and nothing is
# fetched or installed at build time. (Update the path if the libfmt
# dependency version changes.)
configure.args-append \
                    -DBISON_EXECUTABLE=${prefix}/bin/bison \
                    -DFLEX_EXECUTABLE=${prefix}/bin/flex \
                    -DTCL_LIB_PATHS=${prefix}/lib \
                    -DUSE_TCL_READLINE=OFF \
                    -Dfmt_DIR=${prefix}/lib/libfmt10/cmake/fmt

# CUDD improves conditional-arc, constant- and power-propagation handling and
# is enabled by default. CUDD installs cudd.h into ${prefix}/include and
# libcudd into ${prefix}/lib, which is exactly where OpenSTA's FindCUDD.cmake
# looks when handed -DCUDD_DIR=${prefix}.
variant cudd conflicts basic description {Build against the CUDD decision-diagram package} {
    depends_lib-append      port:cudd
    configure.args-append   -DCUDD_DIR=${prefix}
}

# Opt out of CUDD: `port install OpenSTA +basic`.
variant basic conflicts cudd description {Build without CUDD (fewer optimizations)} {}

default_variants    +cudd

# tclreadline (MacPorts port:tclreadline) adds command-line history and editing
# to the interactive `sta` shell. Irrelevant to batch/scripted runs, so it is
# off by default; enable with `port install OpenSTA +readline`. The base
# -DUSE_TCL_READLINE=OFF above is overridden here (cmake takes the last value).
variant readline description {Interactive command-line editing via tclreadline} {
    depends_lib-append      port:tclreadline
    configure.args-append   -DUSE_TCL_READLINE=ON
}
